Last updated 2010/04/18

The Pascal compiler is the successor to the Z80 compiler created in 1980. The 
compiler and its support tooling, which is extensive, lies here. However it also
relies heavily on the assembly language toolset, which is located elsewhere.

At this writing, the compiler supports 80386 and up processors, Windows, linux
and standalone environments.

================================================================================

                         CONTENTS OF PASCAL COMPILER AREA

================================================================================

readme.txt         - This file.

ch2ph              - Contains a converter for C header files to Pascal header 
                     files.
          
check_encoder      - Contains the check encoder. This is an obsolete program
                     that was used to bring up the first I80386 compiler.
          
common_libraries   - Contains several support libraries for pascal that are not
                     machine dependent, such as paslib, strlib and several 
                     others.
          
c_parser           - Contains a front end only for the C language. This was used
                     as the basis of the ch2ph tool. A dead project now, since
                     there is no intention of integrating C into the IP 
                     platform.
          
document           - Contains the compiler manual, various documents.
          
dump_intermediate  - Intermediate dump program. This is a utility to produce a
                     formatted of Pascal intermediate files.
          
encoder            - Contains the production encoder. The encoder is presently
                     targeted at I80386 and up CPUs. However, it is general
                     purpose and will be extended.
          
ibmpc              - Contains facillities to run Pascal directly on the IBM-PC
                     as an embedded program.
          
ide                - Contains the integrated development environment for Pascal.
          
installer          - Contains the graphical installer for Pascal.
          
common_headers     - Contains a series of header files for common library 
                     components. No actual code is contained, just header files.
                     The names will be the same as various modules around the
                     system.
          
parser             - Contains the front end for the compiler.
          
simulator          - Contains the CPU independent intermediate code simulator.
          
test               - Contains various testing files, including the test suite.
                     Note however, that several programs use their own testing 
                     programs in the same directory as the program.
          
pretty_printer     - The start of a prettyprinter program for pascal. 
          
pc                 - Contains pc, which is the command line tools integrator for
                     Pascal. It calculates a dependency tree for a program, and 
                     runs all of Pascal utilities.
          
unix_platform      - Contains the support library implementation for Unix/Linux.
          
windows_platform   - Contains the support library implementation for Windows.

freedos32_platform - Contains the support library implementation for freedos32.

================================================================================

Common methods and procedures used here:

================================================================================

build.bat  - Build program(s) in the given directory

update.bat - Copy binaries and any other data to the run tree and release tree

clean.bat  - Remove transient (automatically built) files

Each directory has a "build.bat" file that will cause the main program in the
directory to be built. Each directory also has an "update.bat" file that copies
the binaries and any assocated files to the IP run area, usually c:/ip/...

Most directories have a readme.txt file that explains more about the specific
program the directory contains.
